|~ `::quote`, the kind of code held as a value, and what is declared
   under it: its constructor and its refusals, the refusal of text read
   as code and the facts it carries, and the verbs that read a quote. A
   quote is also the vector of its steps, so the verbs of vectors read
   it as one, and a quote under a tag, a link or a snippet of a page,
   reads them through the walk of its tags.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence], [QSQUO#held]. ~|

:quote
  |~~ A quote is code held as a value, `~(add 1 | mul 2)`: steps that
      run only when something runs them. A verb that runs code later
      takes a quote, `map ~(mul 2)`, `filter ~(gt 1)`, `sort /name`,
      and `apply` runs one on a subject, `5 | apply ~(add 1)` answering
      `6`. A quote written in the step that hands it to a verb reads the
      names of that step wherever the verb runs it,
      `{:bonus 10} | with [/bonus] ~([1 2] | map ~(add bonus))` answering `[11 12]`; one held as
      data, in a name, a field or a page, runs in a lane of its own and
      reads no value the reader named, and `fill` writes the values it
      needs at its head, `~(mul rate) | fill {:rate 1.2}`.

      It is written `~(…)` around a pipeline, and one name drops the
      parentheses, `~count`. A verb that runs code takes a path bare,
      `sort /name`, so a quote of a path alone is refused where it is
      written; one built, by `parse` or by `tag ::quote`, answers the
      path and prints under its tag, `::quote[/name]`. A
      quote is the vector of its steps under `::quote`,
      `~(add 1 | mul 2) | count` answering `2`, and each step reads as
      data: a literal as itself, and a step
      that computes as a record, `~(add 1) | first` answering
      `::call{:name :add :modifiers [1]}`. `parse` turns text into a
      quote, `toString` a quote into the text of its code, and JSON
      writes a quote as its print.

      Because code is a value, a page holds links and snippets that run,
      the laws of a name are quotes that answer true, and the trace of a
      failure holds the steps it skipped, a quote that runs again as held
      code runs.
      ::link~(::call) ::link~(::any/apply)
      ::link~(::call/ValueWithheldError) ::link~(::qlang/code)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts
        |~~ A vector whose every element is a step, built into the quote.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::quote` builds code from the vector of its steps; a payload
            of another kind is refused, a vector under another tag among
            them, which `payload` opens.
            ::link~(::quote)
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementNotStepError
        |~~ Every element of a quote is a step: a literal, a path, a
            quote, a vector, a set or a map of steps, or a step as data,
            `::call`, `::bind` and their kin. A value with no reading as
            code, a value under a tag of another kind among them and the
            quote of a path alone, which a step writes bare, is refused at
            the index it stands at.
            ::link~(::quote)
        ~~|
    | throws :StepNotCommandError
        |~~ The code of a quote is its declarations and the query after
            them, and a step of the query after its first is a command, a
            call or a group: a value stands at the head of the query, where
            it is the subject, and after `!|`, where it is the recovery, and
            a declaration stands at the head of the code, none after the
            first step of the query nor on the fail track. The step at
            `:index` is refused, and `:message` names the verb that takes a
            value, `get` for a path and a literal, `tag` for a tag and
            `apply` for a quote, or the head where a declaration stands. A
            value a step answers is built by a verb, `get [1 2]`, and a value
            under a tag by `tag`.
            ::link~(::quote) ::link~(::qlang/pipeline)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :message
              |~~ What the rule says of the value, the verb that takes it. ~~|
              ::string)
    | throws :LiteralComputesError
        |~~ A literal computes nothing: the step at `:index` is a literal
            that holds a name or a group, which the shape `get` builds
            computes, and `:message` writes that template, its parts
            computed written as quotes, `get [~(count)]`.
            ::link~(::quote) ::link~(::any/get)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :message
              |~~ What the rule says of the literal, the template `get` builds it
                  from.
              ~~|
              ::string)
    | throws :ParseError
        |~~ The source does not read as qlang: `:marker` puts a caret
            beneath `:source` under the character where the reading
            stopped, `:location` names that span, its line and column read
            by `line` and `column` of its `start`, `:message` names the habit a form of
            another language wrote, the step a combinator misses and what a
            source cut short left open, a bracket, a string, a quote, a doc
            or a comment, and beside it `:expected` lists what could stand
            there where the reading went no further.
            ::link~(::quote) ::link~(::span)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :source
              |~~ The line of the text where the reading stopped, cut around
                  that point by an ellipsis where its print would pass the
                  room of a fact of an alert, three hundred characters.
              ~~|
              ::string
          | wires :marker
              |~~ A caret under the character of `:source` where the reading
                  stopped and a tilde under each other character of its span
                  there, spaces before them and after them to the end of
                  `:source`, each character counted as its print writes it,
                  so the two print in one column.
              ~~|
              ::string
          | wires :message
              |~~ What the grammar says where the reading stopped, a bracket
                  left open among it.
              ~~|
              ::string
          | wires :expected
              |~~ The continuations the reading could take where it stopped: each
                  the text of a literal or a part of the grammar named as a
                  keyword, `:name`, `:whitespace`, `:end-of-source`, beside
                  what it met as `:found`.
              ~~|
              ::vec
          | wires :found
              |~~ What the reading met where it stopped, none at the end of the
                  source.
              ~~|
              ::string
          | wires :location
              |~~ The span of the text where the reading stopped: null where the
                  reading stopped at no point of it.
              ~~|
              ::span
          | wires :origin
              |~~ Where the text came from, the source, the cell or the verb that
                  read it.
              ~~|
              ::keyword)
    | proves :countsItsSteps
        |~~ A quote of three steps counts three. ~~|
        ~(~(1 | add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 3)
    | proves :printsADeclarationALine
        |~~ A quote of declarations alone prints a declaration a line. ~~|
        ~(~(:rate 2; :fee 5) | toString | eq ":rate 2;\n:fee 5;")
    | proves :equalsItsCopy
        |~~ Two quotes of one code are equal. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | eq ~(add 1))
    | proves :printsAPathAloneUnderItsTag
        |~~ A quote of a path alone, built, prints under its tag. ~~|
        ~("/name" | parse | print | eq "::quote[/name]")
    | proves :answersThePathItHoldsAlone
        |~~ A quote of a path alone answers the path, and reads nothing. ~~|
        ~({:name 1} | apply ("/name" | parse) | eq /name)
    | proves :buildsBackFromItsSteps
        |~~ The steps of a quote build it back. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | payload | tag ::quote | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))
    | proves :filtersItsSteps
        |~~ A quote filters as the vector of its steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(get /name | eq :mul) | toString | eq "mul 2")
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::quote{:not :steps} !| get [~(tag) ~(found | tag)] | eq [::quote/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::map])
    | proves :ElementNotStepError/refusesAValueWithNoReading
        |~~ A value with no reading as a step is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([::Box[1]] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) ~(index)] | eq [::quote/ElementNotStepError 0])
    | proves :ElementNotStepError/refusesTheQuoteOfAPathAlone
        |~~ The quote of a path alone, which a step writes bare, is refused
            at its index.
        ~~|
        ~(get [1 ~("/name" | parse)] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) ~(index)] | eq [::quote/ElementNotStepError 1])
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/refusesAValueAfterTheHead
        |~~ A value after the first step is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([[1] [2]] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) ~(index)] | eq [::quote/StepNotCommandError 1])
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/namesTheVerbThatTakesThePath
        |~~ A path after the first step names the verb that reads it. ~~|
        ~([1 /price] | tag ::quote !| message | contains "write `get /price`")
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/keepsAValueAtTheHead
        |~~ A value at the head is the subject the steps after it run on.
        ~~|
        ~(get [[1 2] ~(~(count) | first)] | tag ::quote | apply it | eq 2)
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/keepsAValueAfterADeclaration
        |~~ A value after the declarations is the subject the steps after it
            run on.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(~(:rate 2;) | first) 5] | tag ::quote | apply it | eq 5)
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/refusesADeclarationInTheQuery
        |~~ A declaration after the first step of the query is refused. ~~|
        ~(get [5 ~(~(:rate 2;) | first)] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) ~(index)] | eq [::quote/StepNotCommandError 1])
    | proves :LiteralComputesError/refusesANameInAVector
        |~~ A vector that holds a name is refused, naming the template
            `get` builds.
        ~~|
        ~([[::call{:name :count}]] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) ~(message)] | eq [::quote/LiteralComputesError "a literal computes nothing: a shape computed is built by `get`, its parts paths and quotes, write `get [~(count)]`"])
    | proves :ParseError/refusesASourceCutShort
        |~~ A source cut short after a pipe is refused. ~~|
        ~("42 |" | parse !| tag | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :ParseError/namesTheLineAndTheColumnWhereTheReadingStopped
        |~~ The start of `:location` reads the line and the column where the
            reading stopped, under the line `:source` holds.
        ~~|
        ~("[1 2" | parse !| get [~(source) ~(location | start | get [~(line) ~(column)])] | eq ["[1 2" [1 5]])
    | proves :ParseError/putsACaretBeneathTheLine
        |~~ `:marker` puts a caret under the character of `:source` where
            the reading stopped and a tilde under each other of its span,
            spaces to the end of the line.
        ~~|
        ~("[mul(2) add(1)]" | parse !| get [~(source) ~(marker)] | eq ["[mul(2) add(1)]" "    ^~~        "])
    | proves :ParseError/countsEachCharacterAsItPrints
        |~~ A quote, which a string prints escaped, counts two characters
            before the caret, so it stands under its character in the
            print.
        ~~|
        ~("{\"a\": 1 \"b\": 2}" | parse !| marker | eq "          ^~       ")
    | proves :ParseError/namesTheContinuationsInTheWordsOfTheLanguage
        |~~ A continuation is the text of a literal or a part of the grammar
            named in the words of the language.
        ~~|
        ~(":" | parse !| expected | eq ["\"" :name])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:quote/toString
  |~~ Answers the text of the quote's code, a string, its steps without
      the brackets around them, `~(add 1 | mul 2) | toString` answering
      `"add 1 | mul 2"`, which `parse` reads back as the quote, so a quote
      turned into text and back is itself, `~(add 1) | toString | parse`
      answering `~(add 1)`. The steps are written in the one spelling the
      printer writes, so the spaces and the line breaks the quote was
      written with are not kept, `"5 |  mul   2" | parse | toString`
      answering `"5 | mul 2"`. A link or a snippet out of a page answers
      the query it holds, without its tags, and the empty quote the empty
      string; `print` writes the quote with its brackets.
      ::link~(::string/parse) ::link~(::any/print)
      ::link~(::quote/answer)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The text the quote reads back from. ~~| ::string
    | proves :writesTheTextOfItsCode
        |~~ A quote answers the text of its code. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | toString | eq "add 1")
    | proves :readsBackAsTheQuote
        |~~ The text parsed again is the quote. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | eq (toString | parse))
    | proves :writesOneSpelling
        |~~ The spaces the quote was written with are not kept. ~~|
        ~("5 |  mul   2" | parse | toString | eq "5 | mul 2")
    | proves :leavesItsBracketsToPrint
        |~~ `print` writes the quote with its brackets. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | get [~(toString) ~(print)] | eq ["add 1" "~(add 1)"])
    | proves :leavesTheTagsOfALink
        |~~ A link answers the query it holds, without its tags. ~~|
        ~(::link~(1 | add 1) | toString | eq "1 | add 1")
    | proves :answersNothingForTheEmptyQuote
        |~~ The empty quote writes the empty string. ~~|
        ~(~() | toString | eq "")
    | impl ::builtin{};

:quote/toVec
  |~~ Answers the vector of the quote's steps, the payload beneath its
      tag, `~(add 1 | mul 2) | toVec | count` answering `2`, as `toVec`
      of a set answers its elements.
      ::link~(::set/toVec) ::link~(::any/payload)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The vector of the steps. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :answersTheVectorOfItsSteps
        |~~ A quote answers the vector of its steps, under a tag of its own
            as well.
        ~~|
        ~([~(add 1 | mul 2) ::Box~(add 1)] | map ~(toVec | get [~(tag) ~(count)]) | eq [[::vec 2] [::vec 1]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:quote/answer
  |~~ Answers what the code the quote holds answers, run as a query
      beneath whatever tags stand over it, `~(1 | add 1) | answer`
      answering `2`, as the `answer` of a cell a host keeps reads what the
      cell answered: a print reads back as its value by `parse | answer`,
      and a snippet answers what it shows. The code is held as data and runs in a lane
      of its own: it reads the verbs of the tree and the names it binds,
      which stay inside it, and no value the reader named,
      `{:rate 0.2} | with [/rate] ~(~(100 | mul rate) | answer)` refused
      under `rate`. It runs from null, not from the subject of `answer`:
      code that reads a subject, `~(add 1)`, is refused on null, and a
      quote meant for a subject runs against it by `apply`,
      `5 | apply ~(add 1)`. Text is read as a quote first by `parse`,
      `"1 | add 1" | parse | answer`, and a link opens the page it names
      by `open`.
      ::link~(::any/apply) ::link~(::link/open) ::link~(::cell)
      ::link~(::doc/snippets) ::link~(::qlang/code)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ What the code answers, a value of any kind. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectNotQuoteError
        |~~ `answer` runs a quote, `~(1 | add 1) | answer`: text is read
            as a quote first by `parse`, `"1 | add 1" | parse | answer`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheNameALinkHolds
        |~~ The code of a link answers the name of the page it holds,
            which `open` opens.
        ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::link~(::vec/sort) ~~| | links | first | answer | eq ::vec/sort)
    | proves :answersWhatASnippetShows
        |~~ A snippet answers what it shows. ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::snippet~([3 1 2] | sort) ~~| | snippets | first | answer | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :withholdsAValueOfTheReader
        |~~ A value the reader named is withheld from the code: a stored
            query of a tax reads no rate `with` gives the code around it.
        ~~|
        ~({:rate 0.2} | with [/rate] ~(~(100 | mul rate) | answer !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]) | eq [::call/ValueWithheldError :rate])
    | proves :keepsItsNamesInside
        |~~ A name the code binds stays inside it: the stored query of a
            tax names its rate for its own steps alone.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:rate 0.2; 100 | mul rate) | answer | get [~(it) ~(::host | env | has :rate)] | eq [20 false])
    | proves :runsFromNull
        |~~ The code runs from null. ~~|
        ~(~(tag) | answer | eq ::null)
    | proves :runsTextReadByParse
        |~~ Text read as a quote by `parse` answers what it spells. ~~|
        ~("1 | add 1" | parse | answer | eq 2)
    | proves :SubjectNotQuoteError/refusesAString
        |~~ Text is no quote until `parse` reads it, and `answer` called by
            its address refuses it under itself.
        ~~|
        ~("1 | add 1" | quote/answer !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::quote/answer/SubjectNotQuoteError ::string])
    | proves :meetsNoTextUnderItsName
        |~~ Text meets no verb of the name `answer`, which a quote answers
            among the kinds that answer what their code answered.
        ~~|
        ~("1 | add 1" | answer !| get [~(tag) ~(addresses | has ::quote/answer)] | eq [::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError true])
    | proves :refusesAQueryThatReadsASubject
        |~~ Code that reads a subject is refused on null. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | answer !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:quote/fill
  |~~ Answers the quote with the value the call gives for each name the
      code reads written at its head, a declaration each in the order the
      code reads them, `~(mul rate) | fill {:rate 1.2}` answering
      `~(:rate 1.2; mul rate)`, so code held as data, in a name, a field
      or a page, runs with the values it needs, as a step writes into the
      code it hands a verb the values of its scope it reads. A name the
      code does not read, one it declares itself among them, is refused
      beside the names it reads near it, and `fill` of a function writes
      the args of its call into its code.
      ::link~(::fn/fill) ::link~(::call/ValueWithheldError)
      ::link~(::qlang/code)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :names
        |~~ The names the code reads and the value written for each, a map.
        ~~|
        /0 ::map
    | throws :NamesKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The names are a map of each name the code reads and its value,
            `~(mul rate) | fill {:rate 1.2}`.
        ~~|
    | throws :NamesNameNotReadError
        |~~ The code reads every name the call gives, so no name misspelled
            stands at its head unread: `:name` holds the name given,
            which the code does not read or declares itself, and `:nearest`
            the names it reads near it in spelling.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name given, which the code does not read. ~~| ::keyword
          | wires :nearest
              |~~ The names the code reads near it in spelling, `[]` where none is near. ~~|
              ::vec)
    | returns |~~ The code, the values written at its head. ~~| ::quote
    | proves :writesTheValueAtTheHead
        |~~ The rate of a price goes to the head of the code that reads it.
        ~~|
        ~(~(mul rate) | fill {:rate 1.2} | eq ~(:rate 1.2; mul rate))
    | proves :writesTheNamesInTheOrderTheCodeReadsThem
        |~~ The quantity and the fee go to the head in the order the code
            reads them.
        ~~|
        ~(~(mul qty | add fee) | fill {:fee 5 :qty 3} | eq ~(:qty 3; :fee 5; mul qty | add fee))
    | proves :runsWithTheValuesWritten
        |~~ A rule kept as data prices each order with the rate written in.
        ~~|
        ~([10 25] | map (~(mul rate) | fill {:rate 1.2}) | eq [12 30])
    | proves :NamesNameNotReadError/refusesAMisspelledName
        |~~ A name the code does not read is refused beside the name it
            reads.
        ~~|
        ~(~(mul rate) | fill {:rtae 1.2} !| get [~(tag) ~(name) ~(nearest)] | eq [::quote/fill/NamesNameNotReadError :rtae [:rate]])
    | proves :NamesNameNotReadError/refusesANameTheCodeDeclares
        |~~ A name the code declares itself is no name it reads. ~~|
        ~(~(:rate 1.5; mul rate) | fill {:rate 1.2} !| tag | eq ::quote/fill/NamesNameNotReadError)
    | proves :NamesKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ The values are named by a map. ~~|
        ~(~(mul rate) | fill [1.2] !| tag | eq ::quote/fill/NamesKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

